  "extra_prompt_rules": [
    "Diary intimacy is welcome; cut empty scenic drift — every image should reveal inner life or advance feeling.",
    "Prefer concrete sensory verbs over abstract spiritual jargon when both would fit.",
    "Keep short poetic line-breaks when the source uses them; do not flatten verse into prose.",
    "Preserve the first-person immediacy — we should feel we are inside her present moment.",
    "Israel / Hebrew-reader cultural competence: later chapters explain Israeli life, places, holidays, and customs to a foreign (Italian) reader. Hebrew readers already know this culture — do not over-explain the obvious. Drop or compress didactic asides that only exist to teach a foreigner what Shabbat, Kippur, Sukkot, hummus, army slang, place names, or everyday Israeli norms are. Keep the narrator's personal discovery, emotion, and concrete scenes; cut tourist-guidebook gloss when the Hebrew audience needs none. Prefer natural Israeli Hebrew wording over translated foreigner-explanations.",
    "English-in-Italian apparatus: the Italian edition often prints an English quote with a trailing footnote digit (cakes1, War7, Thank you11) and later a 'Traduzione:' Italian gloss. For Hebrew: (1) strip those footnote digits, (2) never keep both the English quote and a תרגום/Traduzione gloss — render spoken dialogue once in natural Hebrew, (3) keep English only for ambient signs/slogans Israelis already read as-is (e.g. BRING THEM HOME NOW, Stop This Fucking War) with no digit and no gloss."
  ],
